<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cholesterol biosynthesis (plants, early side-chain reductase)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>Until fairly recently the biosynthesis of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| in plants was poorly understood and only a few enzymes involved in this pathway had been characterized |CITS:[25217510]|. On the other hand, this pathway has been elucidated in much more detail in animals (see for instance |FRAME:PWY66-5|) |CITS:[21902244]|. |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL Cholesterol| has been found in plants at much lower concentrations than in animals (i.e. 1-2% of the total sterols) but can accumulate to significant higher levels in &lt;i&gt;Solanaceae&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[Whitaker88] [Whitaker91] [Japelt11]|. The more prominent sterols in plants, which have been investigated in more detail are the phytosterols (C24-alkylsterols) |CITS:[Piironen00]| (see |FRAME:PWY-2541|). The observation that |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| level and steroidal glycoalkaloid biosynthesis in potato appeared to be connected |CITS:[Bergenstrahle96]| confirmed previous results |CITS:[Tschesche80] [Heftmann67]| and laid the foundation to studies that implicated the involvement of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| in the biosynthesis of steroidal glycoalkaloids |CITS:[Ohmura95] [23473422]|. Because of their adverse effects in human diet steroidal glycoalkaloids are considered a health threat |CITS:[17090106]|, a fact which in view of the advantageous pharmaceutical properties of steroidal glycoalkaloids resulted in considerable efforts to elucidate the underlying pathways of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL|, |FRAME:CPD-17696| in tomato (see |FRAME:PWY18C3-2|, |FRAME:PWY18C3-3|) |CITS:[22180624] [23788733]| and |FRAME:CPD-9214| and |FRAME:CPD-9211| in potato (see |FRAME:PWY-5666|) |CITS:[McCue05] [16298403] [17157337]|. Furthermore, |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| is an important component of membranes and protective surface layers and gives rise to phytoecdysteroids |CITS:[11393511] [25556315]|. The finding that |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| is usually present in plants in only diminutive amounts but rises to significant higher levels in members of the &lt;i&gt;Solaneceae&lt;/i&gt; family suggests that the biosynthesis of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| in those species might be distinct from that of other plants. For instance, in |FRAME: TAX-4100| the level of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| reaches up to 12% of the total sterols |CITS:[26671544]|. The |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| biosynthetic pathway is the gateway to various steroids and vitamins, e.g. |FRAME:VITAMIN_D3| (see |FRAME:PWY-6076|) |CITS:[23717318]|. Metabolic intermediates such as |FRAME:CYCLOARTENOL| and |FRAME:DESMOSTEROL-CPD| are branching points that either give way to plant C-24 alkylsterols (see |FRAME:PWY-2541|) or |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL|. The flux into different pathways depends on the activity of basically two enzymes competing for those substrates, i.e. the sterol C-24 methyltransferase 1, which steers the flux of carbon towards C-24 alkylsterols and in doing so lowers |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| biosynthesis |CITS:[10852933] [12226510] [12692338]|, and the sterol side chain reductase (SSR2), which has been shown to form respective precursor and |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| by reducing the side chain of |FRAME:CYCLOARTENOL| and |FRAME:DESMOSTEROL-CPD| |CITS:[25217510]|. Using the human 24-dehydrocholesterol reductase (|FRAME: HS03985|) |CITS:[11519011]| as probe and performing BLAST searches against the potato unigene database |CITS:[11125077]| and the tomato database |CITS:[20935049]| resulted in the identification of cDNA&apos;s (SSR2) that encoded sterol side chain reductases able to carry out the reduction of |FRAME:CYCLOARTENOL| and |FRAME:DESMOSTEROL-CPD| to form |FRAME:CPD-4181| and |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL|, respectively |CITS:[25217510]|. The |FRAME: G18C3-13| cDNA of tomato was then used to identify the entire set of cholesterogenesis genes in |FRAME: TAX-4081| and |FRAME: TAX-4100| and this information lead to the entire reaction sequence required for the biosynthesis of |FRAME:CHOLESTEROL| in plants |CITS:[28005066] [26876023]|. It was found that the enzymes of this pathway are often promiscuous and are active in related pathways such as |FRAME:PWY-2541|. This is in accordance with the evolutionary &apos;patchwork&apos; hypothesis, proposing that ancestral enzymes with low substrate specificity develop narrower specificities through duplication and diversification over time, which eventually results in the genesis of new pathways with enzymes of new catalytic properties |CITS:[4207200] [26276843]|. This is exemplified by the C4-demethylation complex formed by sterol methyl oxidases (SMO&apos;s), 3&amp;beta;-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase/C4-decarboxylase (3&amp;beta;HSD), and 3-oxosteroid reductase (SDR) |CITS:[8031136] [21902244] [21147141]|. In tomato and |FRAME: TAX-4100| SMO3 and 4 encode proteins that are specific for cholesterol biosynthesis, whereas SMO1 and 2 encode proteins that catalyze reactions in |FRAME:PWY-2541|. Since both groups of SMO&apos;s participate in a multi-enzyme complexes with the same promiscuous 3&amp;beta;HSD and SDR proteins, the specificity of the SMO component determines in what pathway, |FRAME:PWY18C3-1| or |FRAME:PWY-2541|, those enzyme complexes operate |CITS:[28005066] [26876023]|.</Description>
